#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "f374b567-9a69-57ae-88eb-644ebfc451d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-506" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int  \\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^2+3x-4 = (x-1)(x+4) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} & =\\frac{5x}{(x-1)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =A(x+4)+B(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4) & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-1) \\\\ -20 & =-5B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, by plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1) & =A(1+4)+B(1-1) \\\\ 5 & =5A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4}  = \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)} +\\dfrac{4}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{4}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "fce14198-c8ee-50a6-981d-85df79cce761" :question/math-academy-id "e-507" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "725be603-15ff-5875-b99e-abd4b9f3e038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316416} {:answer/id #uuid "6d687db5-537f-5253-8bd2-1cb184f94320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{121}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d687db5-537f-5253-8bd2-1cb184f94320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{121}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316417} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2b73e0-5c91-54e3-976b-a42f3d542b56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14cfb232-8881-5dc6-aba7-20fb95844fe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-349041" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-19x-4=(5x+1)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4} & =\\frac{15x-39}{(5x+1)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{5x+1}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15x-39=A(x-4)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(4)-39 & =A(4-4)+B(5(4)+1) \\\\ 21 & =21B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(-\\frac{1}{5})-39 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}-4)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ -42 & =-\\frac{21}{5}A \\\\ A & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}=\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(242)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{242}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{121}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ab0f3a-4e41-524f-bd9e-03c46311722b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067332} {:answer/id #uuid "c255a472-9b1a-56de-baec-93fc501ee751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067333} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f7b7a9-f5dc-5e8b-81ea-22c5213943d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067334} {:answer/id #uuid "17efe871-acac-5c1b-affc-45c1fa349a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067335} {:answer/id #uuid "4b41ab18-e879-5684-9158-ed41d186f5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c255a472-9b1a-56de-baec-93fc501ee751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067333} :answer-field/id #uuid "c334a28a-25dc-5066-8861-a6d8339b2304" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f6ebcba-4e39-5954-8ee8-c37c592d0f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49604" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)} & =\\frac{A}{3x+2}+\\frac{B}{2x-3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n13=A(2x-3)+B(3x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+B(3(\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{13}{2}B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(-\\frac{2}{3})-3)+B(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2) \\\\ 13 & =-\\frac{13}{3}A \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}=-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{3}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,b=-1,$ and $a+b=1-1=0.$"}]}
